s  and suddenly there are no people on the  street the gang violence is so extreme  in certain parts of town that people  just don't risk walking around at night  the police are almost militarized in El  Salvador they have this extensive  program in the evening and where they go  out they patrol the streets looking for  evidence of gang activity  what was difficult for me was there was  constant chat about what they're looking  for people talking on the radio not  being able to understand them I had to  rely on kind of body language sense of  urgency how they're moving how they were  driving it's quite scary you are on  completely empty streets you feel the  threat of the police they're all decked  in navy blue holding enormous guns have  you lost any friends for friends killed  we felt patrolling around with the  police like you're sort of peering in  you felt like a bit of a voyeur and you  quickly were trying to imagine what it  was like living behind those closed  doors you felt under the spotlight they  went to a heavy ms-13 area there was a  bar there that was pretty bustling it  was about 2:00 or 3:00 in the morning  everyone was lined up against the fence  men and women and they were quite  intimately frisked and their reaction to  that wasn't kind of shouting and  screaming and saying I can't believe  you're doing this it was just quite  fatalistic really they just say okay the  cops are here out we go arms on your  head they didn't even really need to be  instructed on what to do so it felt like  quite a kind of regular event really  police say they believe that this is a  bar that's used by gangs and they're  searching these people to try and look  for any tattoos perhaps any drugs  weapons to anything that might show  membership to certain groups a big part  we actually thought will never get into  a prison and if we did get into the  prison I also thought okay we'll be  shown the model inmate but we won't  actually be shown the proper gang  members the 